{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1000", "sample_idx": 29, "model_name": "qwen/qwen3.5-397b-a17b", "content": "Raymond III served as the Count of Tripoli from 1152 until his death in 1187 and he played a pivotal role within the Kingdom of Jerusalem during a turbulent era marked by constant warfare with Muslim forces. His early life involved captivity after the Battle of Inab where he spent roughly ten years as a prisoner in Aleppo before gaining freedom through a ransom payment. This long imprisonment deeply influenced his worldview and made him more pragmatic regarding diplomatic relations with neighboring Muslim states. He often advocated for peace treaties rather than reckless military campaigns that could endanger the fragile Crusader states.\n\n*Early Life and Captivity*\nRaymond inherited his title while still a youth yet his actual rule began only after his release from Syrian captivity. The years he spent in chains taught him the Arabic language and gave him insight into Islamic politics which later proved invaluable during negotiations. His experience set him apart from other Frankish nobles who rarely understood their enemies or sought peaceful coexistence.\n\n*Regency and Political Struggles*\nHe acted as regent for Baldwin IV who suffered from leprosy and could not always govern effectively. Raymond faced intense opposition from the court faction led by Guy of Lusignan and Sibylla who favored aggressive policies against Saladin. The count tried to prevent Guy from seizing power because he believed such leadership would lead to disaster for the entire kingdom. His political maneuvering caused deep divisions among the Crusader nobility and weakened internal unity at a critical moment when strong cooperation was essential for survival.\n\n*The Battle of Hattin and Final Days*\nDespite his warnings about engaging Saladin in open battle the Frankish army marched into a trap at the Horns of Hattin in 1187. Raymond commanded one wing of the Christian force but escaped the encirclement while most of the army was destroyed or captured. This catastrophic defeat resulted in the loss of Jerusalem and many other cities to Saladin within months. Raymond retreated to Tripoli where he died shortly afterward possibly from illness or despair over the collapse of the kingdom he tried to save. His legacy remains complex since some viewed him as a traitor while others saw him as the only voice of reason in a court filled with folly. Historians continue to debate his actions yet his strategic mind clearly recognized the limitations of Crusader military power against a unified Muslim enemy.", "logprobs": [{"token": "Ray", "logprob": -8.344685e-06}, {"token": "mond", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " III", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " served", "logprob": -0.0018173867}, {"token": " as", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " the", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " Count", "logprob": -4.768373e-07}, {"token": " of", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " Trip", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "oli", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " from", "logprob": -0.008043304}, {"token": " ", "logprob": -0.039419994}, {"token": "1", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "1", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "5", "logprob": -1.549722e-06}, {"token": "2", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " until", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " his", "logprob": -0.00011099001}, {"token": " death", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " in", "logprob": -5.960466e-07}, {"token": " ", "logprob": -8.3446537e-07}, {"token": "1", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "1", "logprob": -7.15256e-07}, {"token": "8", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "7", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " and", "logprob": -1.9073505e-06}, {"token": " he", "logprob": -0.001918725}, {"token": " played", "logprob": -0.045321845}, {"token": " a", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " pivotal", "logprob": -0.050060753}, {"token": " role", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " within", "logprob": -0.0039809207}, {"token": " the", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " Kingdom", "logprob": -0.094240434}, {"token": " of", "logp